# choom workspace

Plain Markdown files with YAML frontmatter, no database, non-interactive commands, stable output.

## Layout

```
.choom/config.toml         workspace marker, do not edit
meetings/YYYY/MM/          one markdown file per meeting
notes/YYYY/MM/             one markdown file per typed or untyped note
notes/daily/YYYY/MM/       one markdown file per day, named YYYY-MM-DD.md
tasks.md                   one checkbox line per task, at the workspace root
```

## Frontmatter

Every file in `meetings/` and `notes/` starts with exactly these six keys (`id` prefixed `meeting_`/`note_`, `type` `""` when untyped, `tags` `[]`):

```yaml
---
id: meeting_20260728_a1b2c3d4
type: "standup"
title: "Q3 planning"
tags: ["platform"]
created: 2026-07-28T09:14:00
updated: 2026-07-28T09:14:00
---
```

**Links**: an ordinary markdown link, `[text](path#id)` -- `#id` is authoritative and never changes;
the path is computed and repaired on save, so `[Q3 planning](#meeting_20260728_a1b2c3d4)` is enough.

## Tasks

One checkbox line per task in `tasks.md`, metadata in a trailing HTML comment; `[ ]` open, `[x]` done. An optional body is indented lines beneath it, one blank line first:

```
- [ ] send the vendor comparison <!-- id:task_a1b2 tags:procurement links:meeting_20260728_a1b2c3d4 -->

  Need the Q3 comparison before the renewal meeting.
```

## Task mirrors

A checklist item that links to a task, `- [ ] [text](path#task_id)`, is a control surface onto that
task's own `[ ]`/`[x]` state -- not a copy of it. Ticking either one and saving updates the other;
opening a document reconciles it against `tasks.md` automatically, with no repair command needed.
The link tolerates hand-editing: reword the text, reindent the line, move the file -- it is still
found by its `#task_id` fragment, never by line number or wording. A mirror whose task no longer
exists is skipped and warned about, never rewritten and never fatal.

In the editor, `/task <description>` (or `/task.<type> <description>`) on its own line captures a
task from that line's words and replaces the line with the mirror; `choom task add --link <id>`
does the same thing from the command line.

## Commands

```bash
choom meeting new "Q3 planning" --type standup --tag platform
choom meeting list --json --type standup --tag platform --since 2026-07-01
choom note today                       # note new/note list take the same flags as meeting new/list
choom task add "send the vendor comparison" --type followup --tag procurement --link meeting_20260728_a1b2c3d4
choom task list --json --all --type followup --tag procurement  # --done: completed only
choom task show task_a1b2 [--json]                      # print one task and its body
choom task done task_a1b2 [--json]                       # choom task undone task_a1b2 [--json]
choom links <id> [--json] [--direction out|in|both]     # what it points at / is pointed at by
choom links check [<path>...] [--json]                  # report stale/dead links
choom links heal  [<path>...] [--json] [--dry-run]      # repair stale links; dead ones untouched
choom config assistant [<value>] [--json]               # get/set the /ai assistant
```

`--tag` and `--link` are repeatable; an unquoted `#tag` is eaten by the shell, so use `--tag`.
`task done`/`undone --json` add `documents_updated` and `warnings` for mirrors it spliced or could not.
Exit codes: `0` success · `1` not found · `2` usage error · `3` workspace error; diagnostics to stderr, data to stdout, never interleaved.
